There was a time when you couldn’t scroll Pinterest or walk through a newly renovated home without seeing an accent wall. One bold pop of color or a dramatic wallpapered surface—it was the go-to design move that promised to transform a room instantly. But now that trends have evolved (and then evolved again), you might be wondering: Do accent walls still work? Or are they a relic of early 2010s decor?
Let’s break it down.
What Is an Accent Wall, Anyway?
Just in case we’re not all on the same page—an accent wall is a single wall in a room that’s different from the others. It might be painted a different color, covered in wallpaper, adorned with wood paneling, or decked out with a gallery of art or shelving. The idea is to draw the eye, add dimension, and inject personality without overwhelming the space.
Are Accent Walls Out of Style?
Here’s the thing—they’re not out, but they’ve definitely evolved.
The classic “one wall painted fire-engine red while the rest are beige” look? Yeah, that’s probably not making a comeback anytime soon. But that doesn’t mean accent walls are dead. Designers today are using them more intentionally. Instead of being a flashy design trick, accent walls are now about adding depth, contrast, and texture in a way that complements the overall style of the space.
Think: moody navy paneling in a home office, a soft blush wall behind the bed in a minimalist bedroom, or a subtle geometric wallpaper in a powder room. The key is restraint and harmony—not just picking a wall and going rogue.
Why They Still Work
Accent walls still have a lot going for them, especially when done well:
They’re budget-friendly. Want to dip your toes into color or pattern without committing to the whole room? An accent wall is the perfect compromise.
They create focal points. Whether it’s a fireplace, a bed, or a dining nook, highlighting one area can anchor the space and make it feel more intentional.
They offer creative flexibility. From paint and wallpaper to wood slats, murals, and even stone—there’s a lot of room to play.
When to Skip Them
That said, accent walls aren’t always the best choice. In very small spaces, adding contrast can sometimes make a room feel more cramped. And in open-concept layouts, choosing one wall to emphasize can get tricky (where does the accent start and stop?). If you’re going for a very calm, cohesive look, high-contrast walls might also interrupt the flow you’re after.
Modern Takes on the Trend
If you’re still into the idea but want to keep things fresh, here are a few modern ways to approach accent walls:
Texture over color. Instead of bold paint, think board and batten, shiplap, or limewash finishes.
Tone-on-tone. A slightly darker shade on one wall can create contrast without the high drama.
Natural materials. Wood, stone, and brick accents can bring warmth and character without clashing with your color scheme.
Ceiling accents. Flip the script and paint the ceiling instead—it’s unexpected, and when done right, it looks amazing.
Final Thoughts
So—do accent walls still work? Absolutely. But like most trends, the way we use them has matured. Today’s accent walls are less about shouting for attention and more about thoughtful design choices that elevate a space. Whether you go bold or subtle, they can still be a powerful tool in your decorating toolkit—as long as they feel like part of the bigger picture.
And hey, if you still love that cherry red wall in your living room? Own it. At the end of the day, your home should reflect your taste—not just the trends.

